// The macro four corpus files already use, written for the first time — and
// then rewritten against a proposed quoting form, because the first version
// found that there is no way to write a literal AST.
//
// `macros.md` has the design discussion. This file is its test: the same
// macro, written twice, with what each spelling costs on the page.

import { Interface, Declaration, Statement, Identifier } from elp.macros
import { JSON } from .types.value

// FINDING 21 — `pure` holds, and that is the first thing worth checking.
//
// `functions.md` specifies `pure` as a declared bit over a closed set: no
// `@external` call, no spawning, no channel traffic, no read or write of a
// mutable cell. A macro body allocates heavily (every `Declaration` it
// builds), recurses, and mutates locals — all of which are **pure** under
// that definition, which is why allocation was deliberately left out of the
// set. The definition survives its most demanding customer unchanged.
//
// It also confirms the termination point: this body loops over a field list
// of unbounded length and nothing proves it halts, which is exactly why
// `macros.md` needs a step budget rather than a proof.
@macro
export pure fn CodeGen(
	interfaces [Interface],
	node       Declaration,
) -> [Declaration] {
	var out [Declaration] = []

	for face in interfaces {
		match face.name() {
			"Codec" -> out.append(encodeFor(node))

			// FINDING 22 — a macro cannot report a good error, and
			// `diagnostics.md` has no story for one.
			//
			// `@CodeGen(Default)` and `@CodeGen(ToJSON)` are both used in the
			// corpus and neither is implemented here. The honest thing is to
			// reject the unknown name with a message naming the attachment
			// site — and a `pure fn` has no way to emit a diagnostic at all.
			// Returning an empty list silently generates nothing, which is
			// the worst of the three options and the only one available.
			//
			// `macros.md` lists *"diagnostics when macro evaluation fails"*
			// as **not discussed at all yet**. This is the shape it takes:
			// not a panic inside the evaluator, but a macro that wants to say
			// "I do not know how to do that, here" and has no channel.
			_ -> nil
		}
	}

	return out
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FINDING 23 — THE finding, and the reason `macros.md` gained a section.
//
// The first version of this file built its output with constructor calls:
// `Statement.call("fields.set", [...])`, `Declaration.method(...)`. It took
// ninety lines to produce twelve, and halfway through it collapsed — every
// builder it called took **strings**, because there is no way to write an
// expression as data. So the builder route was never an alternative to
// source-text construction; it was source-text construction with extra steps,
// and `macros.md`'s claim that the AST is first-class turned out to be true
// of what a macro reads and false of what it writes.
//
// Below is the same macro against the proposed `quote` form. The form is not
// ratified; this file is what it was tested on.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

pure fn encodeFor(node Declaration) -> Declaration {
	var writes [Statement] = []

	for field in node.fields() {
		writes.append(setField(field.name(), field.identifier()))
	}

	// FINDING 24 — splicing a **list** is what replaces Rust's repetition
	// syntax, and it costs nothing because a macro here is an ordinary
	// function.
	//
	// `quote!` needs `#(#fields),*` because a procedural macro is a
	// token-stream transformer with no loop of its own. elp's macro is a
	// `pure fn`, so the loop is three lines up, an ordinary `for` over an
	// ordinary `[Statement]`, and the quote splices the list in order because
	// of its **type**. One less sublanguage, and the ugliest part of Rust's
	// form never arises.
	// FINDING 28 — the one place this form needs a sigil, and it turns out
	// the corpus wanted the sigil anyway.
	//
	// `quote(Self = node.name(), …)` reads beautifully and is ambiguous:
	// `Self` already names the receiver's type, and in some quoted positions
	// the bare keyword is legal elp, so nothing says whether it is the hole
	// or the keyword. Renaming the hole to `owner` works and throws away the
	// expressive name for one a reader will not find later.
	//
	// `grammar-script-notes.md` adds **raw identifiers** — `` `name` ``,
	// Kotlin's and Swift's spelling — for a reason that has nothing to do
	// with macros: `type` is a keyword inside an `@external` block and the
	// corpus names a field `type` twice, a parameter `type` twice, and a prop
	// `type` four times, the last being `Button(type = .FORM_SUBMIT)`, a
	// built-in element's own API.
	//
	// So the hole keeps its name and carries the delimiter. The position is
	// better than Rust's rather than a retreat to it: Rust marks *every*
	// splice so none highlights, and marking only the keyword collisions
	// leaves the ordinary hole bare and free while the rare one gets a
	// delimiter editors already render as an identifier.
	return quote(`Self` = node.name(), writes) {
		export fn `Self`.toJSON(&self) -> JSON {
			var fields Map<string, JSON> = .new()
			writes
			return JSON.OBJECT { of = fields }
		}
	}
}

// FINDING 25 — a hole needs a **type**, and the reason is that the same
// value splices two ways.
//
// `key` and `name` below are the same field. The first splices as a string
// literal (`"title"`), the second as an identifier (`title`). Rust handles
// this by convention — you pass `#name` and `#name_str` and get it wrong
// sometimes — and the proposal handles it by typing the hole: a `string`
// hole always splices as a string literal, an `Identifier` hole always
// splices as a name. Two parameters, checked, rather than two conventions.
//
// That is the cost of the form and it is worth paying. It is also the reason
// `Field.identifier()` has to exist beside `Field.name()`, which is a
// reflection-API question `macros.md`'s open question 1 did not anticipate.
pure fn setField(key string, name Identifier) -> Statement {
	return quote(key, name) {
		fields.set(key, self.name.toJSON())
	}
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FINDING 26 — hygiene is the real cost of the form, and `JSON` above is
// where it bites.
//
// Every identifier inside a `quote` that is **not** a declared hole is
// literal: `fields`, `var`, `Map`, `JSON`. The rule is one sentence, which is
// why the form is worth having. But `JSON` is imported *by this file*, and
// the code it generates lands in the user's module, which may not import it —
// and `Map` is atmospheric so it always resolves, which makes the failure
// intermittent and confusing rather than reliable.
//
// Every hygienic macro system answers this the same way: a free identifier in
// a quote resolves in the **macro's definition scope**, not the expansion
// site. Scheme does it with syntax objects, Rust with `$crate`. elp would
// need a quote to carry its defining module, which is a representation
// decision on `Declaration` rather than a syntax one — and it is the half of
// quoting that `macros.md` says has not been discussed.
//
// FINDING 27 — and the generic escape does not exist, which is why codegen
// survives at all.
//
// The obvious way to avoid all of this is to not generate code: write
// `Codec<JSON>` once as a **default method** over any reflectable type, and
// let every object conform for free. That is Swift's `Codable` as a user sees
// it, and it needs no macro, no quote and no hygiene.
//
// `interfaces.md` forbids it, in a rule decided for good reasons:
//
//     A default method's body may only call other methods of the same
//     interface — it can never reach into the implementing object's
//     private fields, full stop.
//
// Encoding *is* reaching into fields. So the reflection route cannot be
// routed through a default method, and the macro is not a convenience over a
// generic implementation — it is the only expressible form. That bounds how
// much the quoting ergonomics matter: they are not polish on an alternative,
// they are the mechanism.
//
// The decode direction confirms it from the other side. `fromJSON` has to
// **construct** a `Self` field by field, and `patterns.md` checks an object
// literal exhaustively for an owned aggregate — so there is no partial
// initialisation to build into, which is the same wall Swift hits and the
// reason `init(from:)` is synthesised rather than defaulted. Written here as
// a gap rather than a body, because the honest version needs a builder that
// does not exist.

// vim: ft=kotlin
